Subject: [PATCH 10187/11362] [outer-loop] Preserve in-flight state before
 stopping the conductor

Stopping the loop while we work through gate scoping, the stale flagged-
adversarial stamps, and the verdict_class taxonomy -- all of which touch
machinery the conductor exercises continuously.

There is a second reason. The conductor rewrites ops/.audit_findings_ledger_
state.json, ops/.run_sentinel_state.json and ops/.stop_authority_state.json
constantly, and those files are TRACKED. A test-suite mutation marker naming
them can never go clean while the loop runs, so it permanently refuses every
hook-respecting commit. The conductor is immune because it commits with
--no-verify; the cost falls on everyone else. Stopping the loop clears that
deadlock for the duration.

Committing first rather than trusting a kill not to land mid-write, per the
standing commit-first rule. A python child 19 minutes into iteration 49 will be
killed and retried on resume.

